{"name":"Up in Smoke","hex":"#6e706d","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"up in smoke","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To catch fire and burn.","To be completely ruined, devastated, or reduced to nothingness."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/up_in_smoke"}],"entries":[{"title":"Up in Smoke","blocks":[{"text":"Up in Smoke is a 1978 American buddy stoner comedy film directed by Lou Adler in his directorial debut and starring Cheech Marin, Tommy Chong, Tom Skerritt, Edie Adams, Strother Martin, and Stacy Keach. It is Cheech & Chong's film debut."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/27/Cheech_%26_Chong%27s_Up_in_Smoke.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Up_in_Smoke","wikidata":"Q1381289"},{"title":"Up in Smoke (1957 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Up in Smoke is a 1957 American comedy film directed by William Beaudine and starring the comedy team of The Bowery Boys. The film was released on December 22, 1957, by Allied Artists and is the penultimate film in the series."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/d3/Up_in_Smoke_FilmPoster.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Up_in_Smoke_(1957_film)","wikidata":"Q7898071"},{"title":"Smoke","blocks":[{"text":"Smoke is an aerosol emitted when a material undergoes combustion or pyrolysis, together with the quantity of air that is entrained or otherwise mixed into the mass. It is commonly an unwanted by-product of fires, but may also be used for pest control (fumigation), communication, defensive and offensive capabilities in the military, cooking, or smoking. It is used in rituals where incense, sage, or resin is burned to produce a smell for spiritual or magical purposes. It can also be a flavoring agent and preservative."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Ognisko_ubt_0126.jpeg/330px-Ognisko_ubt_0126.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smoke","wikidata":"Q130768"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Smoke_chemical_composition.jpg/500px-Smoke_chemical_composition.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Smoke_chemical_composition.jpg/500px-Smoke_chemical_composition.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chemical composition distribution of volatile organic compounds released in smoke from a variety of solid fuels[4]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASmoke_chemical_composition.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Diesel-smoke.jpg/330px-Diesel-smoke.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Diesel-smoke.jpg/330px-Diesel-smoke.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Emission of soot in the fumes of a large diesel truck, without particle filters","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADiesel-smoke.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Wildfiretopanga.jpg/500px-Wildfiretopanga.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Wildfiretopanga.jpg/1280px-Wildfiretopanga.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Wildfiretopanga.jpg/500px-Wildfiretopanga.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Smoke from a wildfire","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWildfiretopanga.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedi